"Revision 4 answers its own question and passes every check. The script checker finds no problems, every line and picture is verified against the ESV, and the last three fresh viewers each restated the answer and listed 9\u201310 things they learned.\n\n**Checker:** pass, 0 problems. The answer is stated in the landing, and the last line is a statement. Four things learned each carry a verse. The runtime is about 57 seconds: over the 55-second target, under the 60-second cap, so the narrator needs to run near 158 words a minute.\n\n| Criterion | Score | Evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Answer | 5 | Stated in the landing: \"A flawed hero of faith. His failures were his own. His strength was God's.\" The last line is a statement |\n| Value | 5 | Four things most viewers don't know, each from a verse, plus three more in the caption; the famous story (hair, Delilah, pillars) is under a third of the runtime |\n| Hook | 4 | A famous name and a question that splits people; the cracking pillar adds tension |\n| Open loop | 5 | \"Most think his strength was in his hair\" names the usual answer; \"But three times\u2026\" promises the real one |\n| Concrete | 5 | A lion, Ashkelon, ropes, Timnah, seven locks, a razor, about 3,000 on the roof |\n| Clarity | 4 | \"Later\" separates Delilah from the Timnah wife, and the vow line explains why the shave mattered; earlier viewers stumbled at both |\n| Turn | 5 | \"But blind, he looked to God for strength\" pays off the eyes thread and answers the hair myth |\n| Landing | 4 | Echoes the hook and states the answer; one viewer found it sermon-like for friends outside church |\n| Sendable | 5 | Every viewer named someone to send it to, from church friends to a youth-group friend who calls Samson a jerk |\n| Drawable | 5 | Every line can be shown; nothing graphic |\n| Accuracy | 5 | Six fact-check rounds; every line and picture verified |\n| Tone | 5 | Reverent and curious; frank about the revenge prayer without approving it |\n\n### What changed since the script you rejected\n\n- **The old script never answered.** It asked \"hero or villain?\", retold the story everyone knows, and ended on \"Which do you see?\".\n- **The answer now comes first.** It is stated out loud in the landing, and every line before it builds toward it.\n- **Four things most viewers don't know** carry the runtime, each from a verse. The caption adds three more.\n- **Two lights replace the scale.** A tipping scale implies a verdict reached by weighing deeds. Hebrews commends these people \"through their faith\".\n- **New guards so it can't happen again:**\n    - the checker fails any script with no stated answer, one that ends on a question, or one with fewer than three things learned;\n    - Answer and Value must both score 5 on the rubric;\n    - every fresh viewer must restate the answer and list at least three new things.\n\n### Review rounds\n\n| Round | Fresh viewer said | Fact-check found | What changed |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 5 | Clear answer, 3/5: why would a haircut make the Lord leave? | The Hebrews line must cover the whole list, not the collapse; the caption needs \"the angel of the LORD\" | A line that he was set apart to God explains the shave; the Dagon line moved to the caption |\n| 4 | \"Hero\" asserted, 3/5: the Delilah line dodged what Samson said, and \"the New Testament says so\" felt borrowed | His strength left while he slept (16:19); his hands only feel the pillars while he prays (16:26\u201329) | Samson's own words to Delilah; \"But blind, he looked to God\"; Hebrews 11:34 spoken, with a card |\n| 3 | \"Hero\" half-earned, 3/5: who was the crowd? | \"Still knew\" contradicts 16:20; no prayer is recorded in prison | Added the Dagon celebration; \"Later\" separates Delilah from the Timnah wife |\n| 2 | Clear answer, but \"hero\" not earned, 3/5 | Dawn must be natural light, not the Spirit's gold; the ember stays on Samson | Added that he knew where his strength came from; the Judges 21:25 echo moved to the caption |\n| 1 | The answer was soft, 3/5 | \"Same words\" overclaim on Judges 21:25; a tipping scale implies a verdict by weighing deeds; \"Get her for me\" was said at home, with no woman there | \"A flawed hero\" stated outright; the revenge motive named; two lights replace the scale; the scene moves home to Zorah |\n\n**Still open:** viewers raised in church find \"hero\" debatable, because his last prayer is for revenge. The reel says that plainly and lets Hebrews give the verdict. That tension is also why every viewer said they would send it.\n"
